1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:04,530
Dallas, Texas. September 2000.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,930
A meeting took place in this city that is now retold,

3
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:11,580
again and again, as a business parable.

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,960
According to Marc Randolph, a Netflix co-founder,

5
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:16,660
three Netflix executives came here to

6
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:17,900
meet the leadership of Blockbuster,

7
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,560
the largest name in video rental.

8
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:23,410
We have no transcript of that meeting.

9
00:00:26,310 --> 00:00:28,910
The people who were there remember the day differently.

10
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,020
In Randolph's version,

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,660
Netflix proposed that Blockbuster buy the company,

12
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,640
and that the Netflix team run Blockbuster's business online.

13
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,900
Randolph described the meeting in his 2019 memoir.

14
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,240
He recalls the price that was named,

15
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:49,400
and a reaction he read as dismissive.

16
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:54,820
John Antioco, Blockbuster's chief executive at the time,

17
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,140
disputes that version.

18
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:59,940
In 2022 he wrote that no serious conversations

19
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:02,540
about buying Netflix took place.

20
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:04,680
The figure at the center of the

21
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:07,340
story is fifty million dollars.

22
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:09,340
It comes from Randolph's account,

23
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,480
and it is where most retellings begin.

24
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:14,740
Two recollections. One number.

25
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:18,220
And a question that a single meeting, however famous,

26
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:19,960
cannot answer on its own.

27
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,550
To understand the decision,

28
00:01:22,570 --> 00:01:24,730
start with the business Blockbuster had built,

29
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:27,090
and the Friday nights that built it.

30
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,000
Its strength was physical: walls of tapes and discs,

31
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:35,660
new releases in bulk,

32
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,140
and stores close to where customers lived.

33
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,880
A customer chose a film, showed a membership card,

34
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,020
paid at the counter,

35
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,460
and promised to bring it back by a due date.

36
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,440
Repeated store by store, city by city,

37
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,320
that simple transaction grew into a vast retail network.

38
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,320
By the end of 2004,

39
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:04,920
the company described a network of roughly nine

40
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,320
thousand one hundred stores around the world.

41
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,080
Every rental carried a due date,

42
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,640
and every late return carried a fee.

43
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,720
That small line on a receipt added up.

44
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,780
The company later reported that those extended viewing fees

45
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:28,520
made up about fourteen percent of its rental revenue in 2004.

46
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,720
A system this large is powerful. It is also heavy,

47
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,860
and heavy systems are slow to change

48
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,820
direction when the ground moves.

49
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,850
Netflix approached the same customer

50
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:46,950
problem from the other side: a disc, a paper envelope,

51
00:02:46,970 --> 00:02:47,730
and a mailbox.

52
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:51,800
There was no storefront.

53
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,500
Orders moved through distribution centers,

54
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,840
sorting lines and the postal system.

55
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,800
Customers paid a monthly subscription, received discs,

56
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:05,940
watched them, and sent them back.

57
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:08,600
In this model there were no late fees.

58
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:13,520
Two businesses now served the same evening at home,

59
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:14,740
for the same customers,

60
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:18,740
with very different machinery working behind each of them.

61
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,120
One carried rent,

62
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:25,400
inventory and staff across thousands of locations.

63
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:28,680
The other carried postage, fulfillment,

64
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,620
and the cost of winning subscribers.

65
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:35,230
And it lived online, on early web pages and order forms,

66
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:39,370
years before most viewers expected movies to arrive that way.

67
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:43,640
In 2000, the difference in scale was enormous.

68
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,540
Blockbuster was by far the larger company,

69
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:48,600
and the more established brand.

70
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,640
So was the threat obvious in 2000?

71
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:55,620
Or does it only look obvious now?

72
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:02,330
Blockbuster did respond.

73
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:04,470
It moved into online rental,

74
00:04:04,490 --> 00:04:08,170
with a service launched in 2004 to compete directly by mail.

75
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,720
Now it ran two systems side by side: thousands of stores,

76
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:17,380
and a growing operation built around the mailbox.

77
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:24,000
In January 2005, it made a major change.

78
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:26,440
It stopped charging late fees at its

79
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:28,620
company-operated stores in the United States.

80
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,620
The company's annual report for 2005 describes

81
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:37,340
the financial impact of that decision, line by line.

82
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,060
The company reported that the program reduced rental

83
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:47,200
revenues by more than five hundred million dollars,

84
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:50,160
and cost about sixty million to market and implement.

85
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:56,040
In November 2006, Blockbuster introduced Total Access,

86
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:59,000
its most direct answer yet to the mail-order challenge.

87
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:02,760
Online members could return a mailed disc

88
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,220
at a store and leave with another film.

89
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,000
For the first time, the two systems connected.

90
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,280
Subscribers grew quickly.

91
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:13,980
So did the cost of serving them,

92
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,660
and of the promotions that brought them in.

93
00:05:20,850 --> 00:05:22,690
Growth came with losses.

94
00:05:23,150 --> 00:05:25,150
Attracting customers is not the same

95
00:05:25,170 --> 00:05:27,610
thing as making each of them profitable.

96
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:31,760
Retail stores.

97
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:33,320
Discs by mail.

98
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:36,540
Each with its own costs, its own customers, and soon,

99
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:38,760
a third model that needed neither.

100
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,400
In January 2007,

101
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:46,040
Netflix announced instant viewing on personal computers,

102
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:48,280
starting with about a thousand titles.

103
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,720
It was limited at first.

104
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:54,360
But it pointed toward a way of watching that

105
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:56,840
needed neither a store nor an envelope.

106
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:01,880
Discs by mail,

107
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:04,180
retail stores and streaming were now

108
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,120
competing for the very same evenings at home.

109
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,420
Running a transition is expensive,

110
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:14,780
and the cost fell on a business whose

111
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,320
core revenue was already under pressure.

112
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:23,700
Debt, liquidity and other long-term obligations left

113
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:26,720
the company with less and less room to maneuver,

114
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:28,880
and less time to adjust.

115
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:32,680
The network that had been the company's greatest

116
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:36,000
strength became harder and harder to sustain.

117
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:43,410
On September twenty-third, 2010,

118
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:47,130
Blockbuster filed for Chapter Eleven bankruptcy protection,

119
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:49,490
a decade after the meeting in Dallas.

120
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:52,740
Some stores continued to operate.

121
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:54,340
But the dominant model of the

122
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,500
neighborhood video store had faded.

123
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,700
Which brings us back to the meeting in Dallas.

124
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:06,440
A famous moment, and still a disputed one.

125
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:13,100
Could an earlier deal, different terms,

126
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:16,200
or a partnership have changed the outcome? Possibly.

127
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:18,700
None of these outcomes can be proven.

128
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:24,480
The record points to something more complex: legacy economics,

129
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:27,200
the cost of transition, and financial constraints,

130
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:28,620
arriving together.

131
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:32,340
One meeting is part of the evidence.

132
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,980
It is not the whole record, and it was never the whole story.

133
00:07:42,150 --> 00:07:46,070
Recognizing change is not the same as surviving it.

134
00:07:53,550 --> 00:07:55,630
Every decision leaves evidence.
